Freeze 2 has no unresolved blocker within the agreed concrete-syntax regression-checker scope. The independent actual subprocess rerun produced all 23 expected outcomes: seventeen required rejections, four positive inert-data/cycle controls, and two explicitly documented data-flow limitations. The exact original budget bytes were retained in every fixture; the engine closure limit remains 25 KB.

Reviewed Freeze 2 identity:

- `scripts/report-bundles.mjs`: `7f4f47fec33e97c2f6accb7f159c7d5e5e31994981246af49aa6540da69e9328`
- `scripts/report-bundles.test.mjs`: `3d8d52e01d89a12e52185ec0765f6fdd72ae440cd4915b731c21179dada912bd`
- Unchanged `budgets.json`: `331bd2c7542a00e9b73ace5d834553e914107c102e7050b75233e9c7aeff3c2d`

The first frozen candidate missed four escaped literal module targets because a raw substring check ran before the AST decoder. It also newly admitted three transparent-parenthesis loader forms and two explicit CDN vendor literals previously rejected by the old gate. These actual failures are preserved. Freeze 2 removes the prefilter, unwraps transparent parentheses, and applies the original vendor-name substring coverage to decoded literal loader targets. Every concrete counterexample now fails the gate, while inert receipt metadata, inert templates and text merely describing an import remain allowed.

Independent closure controls still reject eager engine fingerprints reached through commented side-effect imports, escaped local paths and re-exports. Cyclic static imports terminate and are counted once. A full closure re-exporting two individually small high-entropy chunks fails the unchanged combined engine limit. The root's existing source allowlists remain unchanged; the independently reproduced whitespace side-effect import omission is corrected, and the named-import boundary control still rejects.

Execution used Node 22.23.2, physically copied frozen scripts and TypeScript dependencies, and isolated fixture repositories. Gate executions only inspect fixture source; malicious example loader strings were never imported or executed as modules. Original-gate comparisons supplied its expected inline fingerprint so unrelated factoring did not conceal the before/after loader regression. No browser, network, production, account, SDK publication or real site UI action was performed. The ongoing export/hydration browser review was not duplicated.

The gate is not a complete JavaScript execution/security verifier. The explicit aliased-target and computed-target controls remain accepted and are recorded as limitations, not protection successes. Source allowlists retain a lexical checker rather than a general source-language/data-flow analysis. Dynamic imports remain excluded from the measured static initial closure, so this report alone does not prove when a dynamic request is triggered. The marker fingerprint is a regression heuristic, not authenticity or complete engine-code certification. Native behavior and full built-site acceptance remain root's separate evidence. This independent review does not re-claim the root's reported real-site size measurements or its 26 author tests as independently executed.

Evidence: initial and refrozen identities/code, exact diff from site base `c761a49c55d125bca48ff38d81b0a9ff6fd5adcf`, initial failing and final passing actual subprocess outputs, independent scripts, dependency hashes, final source/budget recheck, and `fixture-trees.tar.gz` containing the complete synthetic fixture trees and per-process raw logs. The first six candidate controls, the next seventeen candidate controls with ten original-gate comparisons, and the final 23-case rerun are retained. Runnable originals remain at `/private/tmp/zodiacs-platform-bundle-gate-independent-review`; delivery text files use `.log` suffixes to avoid creating executable product/test copies in repository documentation.
